R-AL6 R-AL7 R-LF2 R-OV2 R-UP7 R-EP8 R-EP9 R-P5 R-P3 R-PH13 R-UM1 R-SO22 R-PP4 R-CC8 R-AL4 (slice 036b, question U3): the probe rounds of the oracle loop (`PickLoop.run!`, `Oracle.step_probed`) with the fake probes. 12 runs of `update` on 12 projects. The packages are those of `probe/un1-units`. acme/pf 0.1.0 is an anchor (`=`), and acme/parser 1.0.0 is an anchor too, except in run 3. So only json is free (R-SP5): its newest candidate is 2.1.0, then 2.0.5 (2.2.0 is too young). Each run has its own compiler id, because a fact is for a pair (hash, compiler) (R-P4). `fx/units` has the answers of the unit probes (no row: it passes). `fx/probes` has the answers of the baseline, check and build probes (the rows are used in order over the whole case, and a run with no row passes). `research/slice-036b-revert/gen-lp1.py` makes the case.
The calls of a run, in order (fix F1: the header of each release of a pair is read first, so the package is fetched before its probe, `random`, `list_dirs` of the package cache, `read_files` of the header; the case `probe/lp2-fetch` has a release that is not in the cache): `db_query` for the key of the candidate, then one `probes 1` for its unit check (the loop: round 1 of the oracle, a unit probe of the chosen release that has no fact), `db_write` with the fact, and the solver step runs again. A unit probe that fails drops the release, and the solver gives the next tuple (R-EP8: a fact decides only when it is real). Then the ladder: it takes the facts of the loop as known, so it probes only the units that the loop did not probe (the baseline and the units of pf and parser), and it does not ask the db again for the keys that the loop asked for (R-AL4).
Run 1 (R-AL6, R-PH13): the unit check of json 2.1.0 passes, and the whole-app check has a new error in `Wire.roc` of acme/json 2.1.0. The failure of the ladder goes back to the solver as a conflict on the release that the blame names: json 2.1.0 is dropped (the line `note: tuple dropped: ...`, a proposal, TW24), the loop probes json 2.0.5, and the ladder runs again and passes. The header gets 2.0.5 (R-AL7). Run 2: the whole-app check crashes. A crash names no release, so the conflict is on the whole tuple (the answer Q2 of the review of slice 036): the next tuple is json 2.0.5.
Run 3 (the conflict is on the named release): `update json parser`, and both are free. The first tuple is json 2.1.0 with parser 1.2.0. The check has a new error in json 2.1.0, so the conflict is on json 2.1.0 and not on the whole tuple: the next tuple is json 2.0.5, and parser 1.2.0 stays (its unit fact is known, so the loop probes only json 2.0.5). A conflict on the whole tuple would try json 2.1.0 with parser 1.1.0 first. Run 4 (R-P5): `probe.max = 5`. The loop probe, the baseline and the 2 units, and the check are 5 probes. The check fails, and the probe of json 2.0.5 in the loop has no budget: exit 12. The probes of the dropped tuple count (the count goes on, it does not start again).
Run 5: the unit check of json 2.1.0 fails with an error in a file of another package (no module of the package is named): a hard fail, so the loop drops 2.1.0 before the ladder (the same fail was exit 10 in `probe/un1-units` run 7, where no other tuple exists). Run 6: the unit check crashes (SIGSEGV): a fault is a fail of the pair (R-P3, R-EP7). Run 7 (R-UM1, interim): the unit check fails in `Yaml.roc`, and the app does not import it. The used modules are known in the ladder only in this part, so a fail with a module touches nothing in the loop: json 2.1.0 stays, and the ladder prints the note about the module (the scan in the loop is a later part).
Run 8 (R-LF2, R-EP9): `--no-probe`, and the db has a hard fail of json 2.1.0 for this compiler. The loop gives the input `Off`, and it asks the db only for the chosen releases that have no row yet: json 2.1.0 has a fail (it is dropped), json 2.0.5 has no row, and `Off` lets it pass. No unit probe runs. Run 9: `--no-probe` and no row: json 2.1.0 is chosen with one db query and no probe. Run 10 (R-P5, R-UP7): `probe.max = 1`. The first unit probe (json 2.1.0, hard fail) uses the budget, its fact is written, and the probe of json 2.0.5 has no budget: exit 12 with the text of `probe.max`, and no write of the project. Run 11 (R-OV2): `[overrides] json = "acme/json@=2.1.0"` is a fixed value, so there is no other tuple, and the unit fail of json 2.1.0 is the answer: exit 10, the text names the unit check, the compiler and the release (`no module is named`).
Run 12 (U3, R-SO22, Q6 of the part): `--no-check` runs no probe in the loop and no db query. The db has a fail of json 2.1.0 for this compiler, and it is not read: json moves to 2.1.0 and the file is written. This is the reading of the design that slice 031 and 036 use (`--no-check` gives no probe at all). It is the user question U3: if the design later wants the lookup with `--no-check`, run 12 changes.
The rekey of a pin with the long month (`nightly-2026-October-13-91fa0c2`) is shown by `probe/pb1-ladder` run 4: the unit fact of the loop has the id of the installed dir, as the facts of the ladder.
